window.MONSTER_CHUNK_114 = [{"name":"Medium Salt Elemental","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"outsider","subtypes":["earth","elemental","extraplanar"],"init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"auras":["dehydration (10 ft., DC 12, 1d6)"],"ac":17,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+7 natural","hp":19,"hd":"3d10+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":1,"will":3,"immunities":["elemental traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to water"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"claw +5 (1d6+3 slashing plus salt poisoning)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"salt poisoning","details":"6 rounds, DC 12"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":10,"constitution":13,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":11,"charisma":11,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmd":15,"feats":[{"feat":"Cleave"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6}],"languages":["Terran"],"environments":["any (Plane of Earth)"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","gang (3-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This crystalline humanoid is slightly translucent and light gray in color. Its head has no discernible features and its power arms end in massive fists.","group":"Elemental","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Dehydration Aura","kind":"Su","details":"A salt elemental's aura draws moisture from an area centered on itself. Living creatures within range take damage (damage is detailed with each elemental) each round they remain in the area. A successful Fortitude save reduces the damage by half. This effect is especially devastating to plants, animals, and aquatic creatures, which take a -4 penalty on their saving throws. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Earth Mastery","kind":"Ex","details":"A salt elemental gains a +1 bonus on attack and damage rolls if both it and its foe are touching the ground. If an opponent is airborne or waterborne, the elemental takes a -4 penalty on attack and damage rolls. These modifiers apply to bull rush and overrun maneuvers, whether the elemental is initiating or resisting these kinds of attacks. (These modifiers are not included in the statistics block.)"},{"type":"Salt Poisoning","kind":"Ex","details":"Anyone hit by a salt elemental's claw attack is sickened for 3 rounds + 1 round per Hit Dice of the salt elemental. A successful Fortitude save negates this effect. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Vulnerability to Water","kind":"Ex","details":"A salt elemental submerged in water that covers at least half its body takes 1d6 points of damage each round until it escapes. Damage from water-based attacks (such as the slam attack of a water elemental) deal +50% damage to a salt elemental."}],"description":"Salt elementals inhabit regions rich in salt upon the Plane of Earth. Some believe they originated on a pocket plane somewhere near the energy planes and Plane of Water; perhaps an Elemental Plane of Salt. Salt elementals, avoid large expanses of water at all costs, knowing exposure to such a large quantity spells disaster.\n\nSalt elementals spend most of their time wandering their realm and often make forays into other planes (except the Plane of Water), seeking creatures on which to feed. They are particularly fond of plants, which are extremely vulnerable to their dehydrating ability.\n\nA salt elemental generally appears as a humanoid with no discernible features, though they can take other forms, and some do, preferring to appear in the shape of large animals or giant vermin (usually beetles).","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Medium Stingray","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"100 ft."}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +3 natural","hp":22,"hd":"4d8+4","hdvalue":4,"fort":5,"ref":5,"will":2,"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"sting +5 (1d4+3 plus poison)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"poison"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":13,"constitution":13,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmd":16,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Ability Focus (poison)"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Stealth","value":5},{"skill":"Swim","value":10}],"environments":["warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","school (2-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A wide, sail-like creature glides through the water dragging a barbed, whip-like tail behind it.","group":"Stingray","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Sting-injury; save Fort DC 15; frequency 1/round for 4 rounds; effect 1 Str and paralysis 1d4+1 rds.; cure 1 save."}],"description":"Stingrays are bottom feeders and are found in warm, shallow waters near coastlines and shores. They sustain themselves on a diet of plankton, aquatic plants, small crabs, and small fish. Larger stingrays, like smaller rays, are found in shallow, warm waters, though these creatures often swim into much deeper waters. Giant stingrays feed on creatures such as fish, giant crabs, octopus, and sharks. They are more aggressive than their smaller counterparts and often engage prey larger than themselves, relying on their poison to bring down a foe.\n\nFemale stingrays are usually larger than males. Young stingrays resemble adult rays, but are much smaller in size. During birth, the young's pectoral fins are folded against its body but open as soon as it is born.\n\nStingrays are completely flat with no discernible head. At one end is a long, whip-like tail that ends in a razor-sharp and serrated barb. Their eyes appear as small bumps on the end opposite the tail and their mouth is located on the underside of their body. The sides of a stingray are composed of large, wide pectoral fins (mistakenly referred to as wings sometimes). Stingrays are brown, black, or slate gray and their underbelly is white. Small stingrays are about three ft. across, while the largest ray measures 12 ft. across.\n\nSmall stingrays are generally inoffensive creatures. Most \"attacks\" occur in shallow waters when a creature steps on a buried ray. If stepped on, the stingray lashes out with its stinger. If threatened by an opponent much larger than it, the stingray attempts to sting its opponent and then flees.\n\nLarger stingrays are more aggressive than their smaller counterparts, and some are very active when searching for prey. They are nocturnal hunters and attack using a tail sting or wing buffet.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Mongrelman","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"LN","size":"Medium","type":"monstrous humanoid","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +4 natural","hp":13,"hd":"2d10+2","hdvalue":2,"fort":1,"ref":4,"will":5,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"club +5 (1d6+4) or slam +5 (1d4+4)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":16,"dexterity":13,"constitution":12,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":14,"charisma":6,"baseatk":2,"cmb":5,"cmd":16,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Survival)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":7},{"skill":"Disguise","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Sleight of Hand","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10},{"skill":"Survival","value":9}],"languages":["Mongrelman"],"sq":[{"type":"sound imitation"}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-5)","band (6-11 plus 1 3rd level sergeant)","tribe (10-100 plus 1 3rd-level sergeant per 10 adults, 1 or 2 bodyguards of 4th or 5th level, and 1 leader of 6th-8th level)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"The hideous creature approaching from the shadows looks to be pieced together from parts of other monsters as some sort of vile joke or blight on Nature.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Sound Imitation","kind":"Ex","details":"Mongrelmen can imitate sounds made by any creature they have previously encountered, including monsters with special vocal attacks. They cannot, however, mimic the special vocal attack powers or damage dealt by such attacks. A successful DC 14 Will save made by a listener detects the falsehood. The save DC is Constitution-based and includes a +2 racial bonus."}],"description":"Though not inherently evil, mongrelmen are shunned from society because of their appearance. They make their homes far from civilization, and those few encountered in settled areas are usually slaves or servants of the local humanoid races. Mongrelmen that must travel among other races take precautions so as not to reveal their true identities, using cloaks, capes, and the like to hide their forms. Mongrelman society is a collection of close-knit tribes, each with its own leader. Mongrelmen never fight against other mongrelmen, preferring to live peaceably with others of their kind, for all mongrelmen know they are shunned by outsiders and must stick together if their race is to survive. Mongrelmen stand about 6 ft. tall. The average lifespan of a mongrelman is 35 years. Mongrelmen speak their own guttural language. Mongrelmen shun combat if possible, only entering melee if ordered to do so by their superiors. In combat, mongrelmen fight with clubs or longswords. Some carry blowguns and needles or darts (the blowgun needles and darts are usually coated with bloodroot poison).","as_character":[{"description":"Mongrelmen possess racial Hit Dice and may have class levels. Mongrelmen have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+4 Strength, +2 Dexterity, +2 Constitution, +2 Wisdom, -4 Charisma: Mongrelmen are physically gifted, but odd to behold"},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Mongrelmen can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"Mongrelmen begin play with 2 levels of monstrous humanoid, which provides 2d10 HD, a base attack bonus of +2, and base saves of Fort +0, Ref +3, Will +3."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A mongrelman's monstrous humanoid levels give it skill points equal to 2 x (4 + Int modifier, minimum 1).Mongrelmen receive the following racial modifiers: +8 Disguise, +4 Sleight of Hand, +4 Stealth."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A mongrelman's monstrous humanoid level gives it one feat."},{"type":"Thick Skinned","value":"Mongrelmen receive a +4 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Natural Weapons","value":"Slam (1d4)."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Mongrelman begin play speaking their own guttural language (Mongrelman). Mongrelmen of high intelligence can choose any of the following bonus languages: Common, Draconic, Elven, Goblin, Orc."},{"type":"Player Characters","value":"Mongrelman PCs may designate their own parts, choosing up to three beneficial monstrous parts. The player may select up to two additional beneficial parts if they chose an equal number of detrimental parts. An ogre or minotaur arm counts as two beneficial parts."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Mongrelman originally appeared in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission.","gear":"club"},{"name":"Monstrous Crab","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"vermin","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +5 natural","hp":19,"hd":"3d8+6","hdvalue":3,"fort":5,"ref":2,"will":1,"immunities":["vermin traits"],"weaknesses":["water dependency"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":20}],"melee":"2 claws +4 (1d4+2 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"claw 1d4+2"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":12,"constitution":14,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":2,"cmb":4,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":15,"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Swim","value":10}],"environments":["temperate aquatic","warm aquatic"],"organizations":["Solitary","swarm (2-12)"],"treasure":"None","description_visual":"Long spindly spider-like legs. Two monstrous claws protrude from the front of its body.","group":"Crab","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Water Dependency","kind":"Ex","details":"Monstrous crabs can survive out of the water for 1 hour per point of Constitution. Beyond this limit, a monstrous crab runs the risk of suffocation, as if it were drowning."}],"description":"Monstrous crabs are omnivores and spend most of their time combing the ocean floors for food. Many act as vegetarians and sustain themselves on a diet of algae, fungus, and water-based plants, while others act as scavengers or predators. Some have been known to actively hunt giant clams and snails (the monstrous crab pries the shell open and devours the fleshy innards). Still others prefer to dwell in coastal waters and prey upon land-based creatures that wander too close to the shoreline. Monstrous crabs are often hunted as food by other races (particularly humans and sahuagin).","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Monstrous Crab originally appeared in the First Edition Monster Manual (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1977) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Monstrous Jellyfish","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"watersense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":12,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +2 natural, -1 size","hp":13,"hd":"3d8","hdvalue":3,"fort":3,"ref":2,"will":1,"defensive_abilities":["jet","transparent"],"immunities":["vermin traits"],"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":10}],"melee":"tentacles +3 (1d6 plus poison and grab)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","strength":11,"dexterity":12,"constitution":10,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":2,"cmb":3,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":14,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Stealth","value":5},{"skill":"Swim","value":8}],"environments":["temperate aquatic","warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-5)","cluster (6-11)","swarm (12-23)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"An enormous jelly-like being floats peacefully in the warm waters, trailing long seaweed-like tendrils from its central mass.","group":"Jellyfish","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Jet","kind":"Ex","details":"A monstrous jellyfish can jet backward once per round as a full-round action at a speed equal to four times its swim speed. It must move in a straight line, but doesn't provoke attacks of opportunity while jetting."},{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Tentacle-injury; save DC 13; frequency 1/ minute for 6 minutes; effect 1d6 Dexterity damage; cure 2 consecutive saves. The save DC is Constitution-based and includes a +2 bonus."},{"type":"Transparent","kind":"Ex","details":"A monstrous jellyfish is nearly invisible floating in the water. An observer must succeed on a DC 20 Perception check to notice the jellyfish."},{"type":"Watersense","kind":"Ex","details":"Monstrous jellyfish can automatically sense the location of anything within 60 ft. that is in contact with water."}],"description":"The jellyfish's body is a translucent pink, blue, or purple hollow form resembling an inverted umbrella of sorts. A giant jellyfish's body averages 8 ft. or more in diameter, while its tentacles (trailing beneath it underwater) can reach lengths of around 50 ft. or greater. Special muscles on the underside of the jellyfish's body push water out of it, allowing the creature to swim through propulsion. Jellyfish eat anything that contacts their tentacles - usually crustaceans, fish, algae, and plankton. A monstrous jellyfish spends its time floating in the oceans and seas. When something contacts its tentacles, it quickly rolls its tentacles around the creature or object, attempting to paralyze and devour it.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Mudman","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"outsider","subtypes":["earth","elemental","extraplanar"],"init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"120 ft."}],"ac":12,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 natural","hp":15,"hd":"2d10+4","hdvalue":2,"fort":5,"ref":0,"will":3,"dr":"5/magic","immunities":["elemental traits"],"weaknesses":["magic"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10}],"melee":"slam +4 (1d4+3)","ranged":"touch +3 (mud bomb)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"engulf"},{"type":"mud bomb"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":10,"constitution":14,"wisdom":10,"charisma":1,"baseatk":2,"cmb":4,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple when engulfing","cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Focus (mud bomb)","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Stealth","value":0,"special":"+16 in mud pool"}],"sq":[{"type":"mud pool"}],"environments":["temperate water","any underground","warm water"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (3-6)","pack (7-12)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"This stocky humanoid is seemingly composed of mud. It has long, thick arms and bandy legs. Its head is round and featureless.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Engulf","kind":"Su","details":"Once per minute as a standard action, a mudman can hurl itself at any creature of its size or smaller within 10 ft. If it succeeds on a ranged touch attack, the mudman immediately makes a CMB check to grapple the target (this doesn't provoke an attack of opportunity) with a +4 racial bonus. If the mudman fails to establish a hold, it drops into an adjacent square and is staggered for 1 round. If the mudman establishes a hold, it flows over its opponent, covering the creature with its muddy body-it can attempt a new CMB check to maintain the \"hold\" on the creature each round that follows as a free action. The creature is effectively blinded and must either hold its breath or suffocate as long as the mudman retains its hold. Each round the mudman maintains its hold, it deals 1d4+3 points of damage as it crushes and constricts its victims."},{"type":"Magic Vulnerability","kind":"Ex","details":"Mudmen are particularly susceptible to dispel magic, as this spell disrupts the magical energies in their bodies. A mudman subjected to dispel magic takes 1d6 points of damage per caster level (Fortitude save for half damage). Transmute mud to rock deals 1d6 points of damage per caster level to any mudmen caught in the area of effect and leaves them staggered until they can find a new mud pool. Other, similar spells may have similar effects."},{"type":"Mud Bomb","kind":"Su","details":"A mudman can hurl globs of its muddy body mixed with its surrounding mud-this attack has a range increment of 10 ft. If one of these mud bombs hits a target, it quickly grows semi-solid and tenacious, causing 1d4 points of Dexterity damage. If the cumulative Dexterity damage from mud bombs ever equals the target's actual Dexterity score, the target remains conscious but is held immobile and cannot take any physical action (although see below) until the mud is removed or the target breaks free. Spell-like abilities or spells with only verbal components may be used if the trapped creature can make a DC 20 concentration check. It's a DC 20 Strength check (made as a full-round action) to pry accumulated mud off and remove all Dexterity damage caused by the attack-a creature that is held immobile can break free with a DC 25 Strength check made as a full-round action (this is the only physical action the creature can attempt while encased in mud). The mud dries and crumbles to dust in 15 minutes, or in 1d3 rounds if a large amount of water (such as results from a casting of create water) is used to wash it away."},{"type":"Mud Pool","kind":"Su","details":"A mudman cannot leave the bounds of the mud pool it dwells in, although these mud pools may be of any size. A mudman's tremorsense extends throughout the entire mud pool, and it gains a +16 bonus on Stealth checks when hiding in a mud pool. A mudman forced out of its mud pool is staggered."}],"description":"Their natural form is that of a pool of mud about 5 ft. in diameter. In this form, they cannot be discerned from normal mud. Mud pools are formed where the Plane of Earth and the Plane of Water commingle in the multiverse, and thus mudmen are born. Occasionally, a vortex opens to a region on the Material Plane where magical waters have stagnated against the land, thereby forming a mud pool. Mudmen pass through this vortex to the Material Plane. Though not evil, mudmen look with disdain on any who trespass in their mud pools.\n\nWhen a living creature enters a mud pool, the mudman forms its humanoid shape (as a standard action) and attacks until the opponent is slain or leaves the mud pool. A mudman attacks by pummeling a foe with its fists or by hurling globs of mud. Mudmen are bound to the pool where they are formed or where they enter the Material Plane and cannot leave their mud pool.\n\nA mudman's natural weapons are treated as magic weapons for the purpose of overcoming damage reduction.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Mudman originally appeared in the First Edition module UK1 Beyond the Crystal Cave (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and later in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission. It was called the \"Mud-man\" in UK1 (note the hyphen)."},{"name":"Necrophidius","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"construct","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +6 natural, -1 size","hp":46,"hd":"3d10+30","hdvalue":3,"fort":1,"ref":4,"will":1,"dr":"5/bludgeoning","immunities":["construct traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d8+3 plus paralysis)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"dance of death"},{"type":"paralysis","details":"1d6 minutes, DC 12"}],"strength":16,"dexterity":17,"wisdom":11,"charisma":12,"baseatk":3,"cmb":6,"cmd":19,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","skills":[{"skill":"Stealth","value":11}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature monster has a long skeletal body topped with a fanged human skull. Its eyes glow with a hellish red light.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Dance of Death","kind":"Ex","details":"A necrophidius can entrance opponents by swaying back and forth. Those within 30 ft. viewing the dancing snake must succeed on a DC 12 Will save or be dazed for 2d4 rounds. This is a mind-affecting effect. The save DC is Charisma-based."}],"description":"The necrophidius appears to be a great skeletal snake topped with a human skull. To the dismay of some (mainly clerics) it isn't undead and therefore cannot be turned or rebuked. The necrophidius is a construct created by an arcane caster to serve as a guardian or assassin. It performs either task with great skill, and being a construct, it never questions its duty as guard or assassin. A typical necrophidius is 10 ft. long. The necrophidius opens combat with its dance of death ability; entranced victims are bitten and paralyzed. Paralyzed victims are either slain by the necrophidius or left to their own accord (depending on the exact instructions given to the necrophidius by its creator). Rattler A variation of the necrophidius, the rattler is constructed from the skeletal remains of a giant rattlesnake. During the creation process, the tail rattle is left intact and magicked to create a confusion effect on those that hear it rattle. The rattler uses the same statistics as the standard necrophidius except it doesn't gain the necrophidius' dance of death special attack. Instead it gains the special attack detailed below. Rattle (Ex) By shaking its tail rattle, the creature emits a rattling noise to a range of 30 ft. Those within the area that hear this rattle must succeed on a DC 12 Will save or be affected as by a confusion spell for 2d4 rounds. The save DC is Charisma-based. Construction A necrophidius' body consists of a human skull and the skeletal remains of a constrictor snake treated with rare oils and powders worth at least 1,000 gp. NECROPHIDIUS CL 10th; Price 7,500 gp Requirements Craft Construct, animate objects, charm person (or confusion if creating a rattler), geas/quest, polymorph any object, confusion (rattler necrophidius only), creator must be caster level 8th; Skill Craft (sculptures) DC 15; Cost 4,250 gp.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Necrophidius originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Pedipalp","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"vermin","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +4 natural","hp":11,"hd":"2d8+2","hdvalue":2,"fort":4,"ref":2,"will":0,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 pincers +2 (1d6+1 plus grab), bite +2 (1d6+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"1d6+1"}],"strength":12,"dexterity":14,"constitution":13,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":1,"cmb":2,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grab","cmd":14,"cmd_special":"+12 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":4}],"environments":["warm land"],"organizations":["solitary","colony (2-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature looks like a cross between a spider and a scorpion. It has six legs and two thin antennae that constantly writhe and wriggle. Two large scorpion-like pincers protrude from just in front of its foremost legs, and a whip-like tail extends from the end of its abdomen.","group":"False Spider","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Pedipalps are also called whip scorpions. The average pedipalp is 5 ft. long but can grow to a length of 10 ft. Poisonous Pedipalp A rare variety of the common pedipalp exists. Rarely encountered, the poisonous pedipalp is a solitary creature. No more than one has ever been encountered at a given time. They don't associate with others of their kind or with normal pedipalps. The poisonous pedipalp uses the same statistics as the normal pedipalp with the additions below. The poisonous pedipalp is CR 3. Poison Cloud (Ex) When threatened, a poisonous pedipalp releases a cloud of noxious fumes in a 20-foot radius around its body. Living creatures within the cloud must succeed on a DC 12 Fortitude save or be nauseated for 1d6 rounds. The poisonous pedipalp can use this cloud three times per day. The save DC is Constitution-based. A delay poison or neutralize poison spell removes the effect from the nauseated creature. Creatures with immunity to poison are unaffected, and creatures resistant to poison receive their normal bonus on their saving throws.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Piranha Swarm","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Diminutive","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic","swarm"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"keen scent"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":16,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +4 size, +0 natural","hp":16,"hd":"3d8+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":5,"will":2,"immunities":["weapon damage","swarm traits"],"weaknesses":["swarm traits"],"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"swarm (1d6)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"distraction","details":"DC 12"},{"type":"killing frenzy"}],"strength":4,"dexterity":15,"constitution":12,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":13,"charisma":2,"baseatk":2,"feats":[{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Swim","value":10}],"environments":["warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-4 swarms)","school (6-11 swarms)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Beneath your dangling ft. swarms a roiling school of fish. Their tiny mouths flash with the glint of sharp, razor-like teeth.","group":"Piranha","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Keen Scent","kind":"Ex","details":"A piranha swarm can notice creatures by scent in a 60-foot radius and detect blood in the water at ranges of up to 100 ft."},{"type":"Killing Frenzy","kind":"Ex","details":"A piranha swarm that detects blood in the water enters a frenzied state the following round, along with all other piranha swarms within a 100-foot radius. Frenzied piranha swarms bite until they or their opponents are dead. A frenzied piranha swarm gains a +2 bonus to damage and -2 AC for 1 minute. The swarm cannot end the killing frenzy voluntarily."}],"description":"Piranhas are deadly carnivores found only in warm waters, such as seas, lakes, rivers, and streams. They are highly aggressive and attack any living creature encountered or detected in the water.\n\nA piranha swarm's diet consists mainly of other fish, though mammals, birds, and amphibians caught in the water serve as a fine meal too.\n\nPiranhas are small, 8 to 12-inch long black or silvery-black fish with large bulging eyes and a tiny mouth lined with razor-sharp teeth. They are generally black or silver with a red underbelly or mottled red spots on their scales. Eye color is usually gray or silver.\n\nPiranhas are related to both quippers and giant caribes.\n\nPiranha swarms hunt during the day and are usually dormant at night.\n\nSome species are ambush hunters and hide in a single clump of seaweed or coral, shooting out to attack prey when it moves nearby. They attack using their razor sharp teeth, biting and gnashing their prey. Once blood is spilt, the piranha swarm enters a killing frenzy.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Plantoid Servitor","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"plant","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+4 natural","hp":22,"hd":"3d8+6 (+3)","hdvalue":3,"fort":5,"ref":1,"will":1,"immunities":["plant traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40}],"melee":"2 slams +4 (1d4+2 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"grab and bite","details":"melee +4, 1d3+2 plus poison"},{"type":"poison"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":11,"constitution":14,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":11,"charisma":1,"baseatk":2,"cmb":4,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Diehard","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Stealth","value":5}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-5)"],"treasure":"special (see description)","description_visual":"These shambling humanoids resemble green-skinned zombies with glowing red eyes, their heads draped with moss.","group":"Plantoid","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Grab and Bite","kind":"Ex","details":"A plantoid servitor that succeeds on a grapple check can make a bite attack against its opponent as an immediate action. This attack uses the plantoid servitor's full attack bonus and deals 1d3+2 damage."},{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Transformative Poison: Bite-injury; save DC 13 Fort; frequency 1/round for 4 rounds; effect special; cure 1 save. On the first failed save, the victim is paralyzed for 1d6 rounds. On the second failed saving throw, moss rapidly grows on all skin surfaces. The victim gets a +2 bonus on the third saving throw, but if the third save fails the victim becomes a plantoid servitor under the control of the nearest plantoid. There is a 25% chance that the victim becomes a new plantoid in a hideous transformation that takes 24 hours. If the victim succeeds on his third saving throw, the moss that grew on him withers and dies and falls away within a few minutes. The save DC is Constiution-based."}],"description":"Plantoid servitors are humans (and their ilk) who have fallen prey to a plantoid. It is possible for a plantoid servitor to operate independently of a plantoid, in which case the eyes will not glow. Plantoid servitors acting independently from one of the plantoid masters are capable of following only the simplest of instructions, such as \"attack anyone entering this room,\" and the plantoid isn't able to give further instructions until it can once again gain physical contact with the plantoid servitor.\n\nPlantoid servitors drop any held equipment when they are first dominated, but retain any worn gear such as armor, backpacks, capes, rings, and so on. At the GM's discretion, a plantoid servitor may still be wearing-and gaining the full benefit of-any special worn gear or magic items it possessed in its previous life.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Originally appearing in Rappan Athuk (© Frog God Games, 2012)","gear":"special (see description)"},{"name":"Poisonous Frog Swarm","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Diminutive","type":"animal","subtypes":["swarm"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":15,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +4 size, +0 natural","hp":18,"hd":"4d8","hdvalue":4,"fort":4,"ref":5,"will":2,"immunities":["weapon damage","swarm traits"],"weaknesses":["swarm traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10}],"melee":"swarm (1d6 plus poison)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"distraction","details":"DC 12"}],"strength":4,"dexterity":12,"constitution":11,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":4,"baseatk":3,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":5},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":21}],"environments":["any swamps","temperate forests","warm forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-4 swarms)","colony (7-12 swarms)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Each frog in the hopping army bears black stripes on its hind legs and sickly, yellowish eyes.","group":"Poisonous Frog","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Swarm-injury; save Fort DC 12; frequency 1/round for 2 rounds; effect 1d3 Str; cure 1save. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Poisonous frog swarms are composed of small, fierce, poisonous frogs.\n\nThe swarm moves collectively, hopping or jumping toward their prey.\n\nSingle poisonous frogs mate during the second half of the year. The male attracts a female through a series of unique mating calls consisting of strange guttural sounds. When a female answers the call, she lays a clutch of 1d6 eggs in a damp, dark area covered with leaves. The male fertilizes the eggs and protects them during their incubation period. Two weeks later the eggs hatch and the male carries the tadpoles to the water on its back. Tadpoles reach maturity in two to three months.\n\nA single poisonous frog is a small dark green frog with black bands or stripes on its hind legs. These stripes function as a warning to predators that the frog is poisonous. The skin of a poisonous frog is very smooth to the touch. The middle digit on each of its extremities is slightly shorter than the others.\n\nPoisonous frog swarms attack by engulfing their prey and subjecting it to the frog's deadly poison. Creatures that begin their turn in a poisonous frog's space suffer swarm and poison damage.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Poltergeist","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"LE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","subtypes":["incorporeal"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"auras":["unnatural aura 30 ft."],"ac":13,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 deflection, +1 Dex, +0 natural","hp":19,"hd":"3d8+6","hdvalue":3,"fort":3,"ref":2,"will":4,"defensive_abilities":["incorporeal","natural invisibility","rejuvenation"],"immunities":["undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"fly","speed":20,"maneuverability":"good"}],"melee":"telekinesis (see below)","ranged":"telekinesis (see below)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"fear"},{"type":"telekinesis"}],"dexterity":13,"intelligence":5,"wisdom":12,"charisma":15,"baseatk":2,"cmb":2,"cmd":15,"feats":[{"feat":"Ability Focus (fear)"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":5},{"skill":"Perception","value":9}],"languages":["Any one"],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","haunting (4-7)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A presence seems to haunt this place.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Fear","kind":"Su","details":"A creature hit by a thrown object (see telekinesis below) must succeed on a DC 15 Will save or flee in terror for 2d6 rounds. A creature that successfully saves is immune to the fear effect of the same poltergeist for 24 hours. The save DC is Charisma-based."},{"type":"Natural Invisibility","kind":"Su","details":"This ability is constant, allowing a poltergeist to remain invisible even when attacking. This ability is inherent, cannot be dispelled or negated, and isn't subject to the invisibility purge spell."},{"type":"Rejuvenation","kind":"Su","details":"Like ghosts, it's difficult to destroy a poltergeist through simple combat: the \"destroyed\" spirit restores itself in 2d4 days. Unlike ghosts, powerful spells such as dispel evil usually are effective. Short of that, however, the only way to permanently destroy a poltergeist is to determine the reason for its existence and set right whatever prevents it from resting in peace. The exact means varies with each spirit and may require a good deal of research, and should be created specifically for each different poltergeist or group of poltergeists by the GM."},{"type":"Telekinesis","kind":"Su","details":"A poltergeist can create a telekinetic effect to hurl one object or creature within 10 ft. to a distance of 60 ft. (no range increment). This ability functions as the violent thrust version of the telekinesis spell (caster level 10th) with the following exceptions: the poltergeist can hurl only one object or creature per round; the poltergeist uses its Charisma modifier (usually +2) to modify its base attack bonus. A creature targeted by this ability can make a DC 13 Will save to avoid being hurled by the poltergeist. The save DC is Charisma-based."},{"type":"Unnatural Aura","kind":"Su","details":"Both wild and domesticated animals can sense the unnatural presence of a poltergeist at a distance of 30 ft. They will not willingly approach nearer than that and panic if forced to do so; they remain panicked as long as they are within that range."}],"description":"Poltergeists are undead spirits that haunt the area where they died. A poltergeist has no material form and cannot manifest on the Material Plane. Most poltergeists are evil, as they are \"trapped\" in the area where they were killed and can never leave this area unless they are destroyed.\n\nThis \"prison\" drives them mad and they come to hate all living creatures.\n\nPoltergeists cannot manifest on the Material Plane and attack by picking up objects and hurling them at opponents.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Poltergeist originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Ravager Beetle","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"vermin","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":19,"ac_mods":"+9 natural","hp":26,"hd":"4d8+8","hdvalue":4,"fort":6,"ref":1,"will":1,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"fly","speed":20,"maneuverability":"poor"}],"melee":"bite +6 (1d8+4 plus poison and grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":16,"dexterity":10,"constitution":14,"wisdom":10,"charisma":6,"baseatk":3,"cmb":6,"cmd":16,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":-4}],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","cluster (3-8)","nest (9-20)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature is covered in a jet black carapace with whitish markings crisscrossing its back and gold-tinted wing covers.","group":"Beetle","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Gnaw","kind":"Ex","details":"A ravager beetle that is grappling a foe and chooses to damage the foe with an additional grapple check deals twice its normal bite damage (2d8+8 points of damage for most ravager beetles), in addition to injecting an additional dose of poison with each successful check."},{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Bite-injury; save DC 14 Fort; frequency 1/ round for 6 rounds; effect 1d2 Constitution damage and excruciating pain (-2 penalty on attack rolls, checks, and saves); cure 1 save. Multiple bites don't result in cumulative penalties to attack rolls, checks, and saves. Apply the penalties only once for the duration of the poison. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Ravager beetles are omnivorous beetles found in temperate or warm forests, hills, and swamps. While generally sustaining themselves on a diet of foliage and grasses, they sometimes scavenge the remains of creatures killed by other predators.\n\nLike most beetles, a ravager has a thick plated carapace and two large mandibles it uses to crush and chew its food. Its carapace is black in color with several white streaks crisscrossing it. Its mandibles are dark bluish-black. Its wing covers are black with hints of gold. A typical ravager beetle is about 4 ft. long.\n\nRavagers have a single life cycle that spans an entire year. Females generally lay 4-8 eggs in soft earth or soil and within two weeks the larva emerges. Young are almost always born in the warmer spring and early summer months. Young are noncombatants and rely solely on their mother for protection and food, feeding generally for 10 days before entering the pupa stage. After about 20 days, the pupae become adults.\n\nRavager beetles are generally scavengers by nature, and rarely attack, except in times when food is scarce. Even then, they usually limit their attacks to weakened, sleeping, wounded, or otherwise incapacitated prey. When attacking, ravager beetles lock onto an opponent with their mandibles and continue biting and crushing the target until it is dead.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Redcap","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"CE","size":"Tiny","type":"fey","init":8,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":16,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +2 natural, +2 size","hp":4,"hd":"1d6+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":2,"ref":8,"will":7,"dr":"5/cold iron","sr":16,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"tiny short sword +6 (1d3-2 plus poison) or 2 claws +6  (1d2-2)","ranged":"dagger +6 (1d2-2 plus poison)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","spell_like_abilities_caster_level":7,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":8,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"invisibility"}]},{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"dancing lights"},{"spell":"detect good"},{"spell":"glitterdust"},{"spell":"mirror image"},{"spell":"ventriloquism"}]}],"strength":7,"dexterity":19,"constitution":12,"intelligence":15,"wisdom":14,"charisma":14,"baseatk":0,"cmb":7,"cmd":21,"feats":[{"feat":"Dodge","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Bluff","value":6},{"skill":"Craft (poisonmaking)","value":6},{"skill":"Craft (any one)","value":6},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8,"special":"+12 in forests"}],"languages":["Common","Elven","Sylvan"],"sq":[{"type":"poison use"}],"environments":["temperate forests","any underground","warm forests"],"organizations":["gang (2-4)","band (5-12)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"An angry looking fairy, just about 20 inches tall, shrieks and waves a long, wool hat soaked entirely in blood. His wrinkled gray skin, silvered hair, and gray blue eyes give him the appearance of a miniature old man. His snarling mouth reveals small upward curving fangs, while its hands end in eagle-like talons.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison Use","kind":"Ex","details":"A redcap is never at risk of poisoning itself when handling poison. They usually use aconite poison on their daggers."}],"description":"Redcaps are evil relatives of the brownie that dwell in ruins, dungeons, and other such desolate places. They are not averse to taking up residence near civilized lands, and some actually prefer it, as it simply widens the grounds in which they can work their vile magic.\n\nRedcaps hate humans and most other good-aligned fey creatures (especially brownies, grigs, and pixies). Though not combat-minded, they often go out of their way to kill good-aligned fey if given the chance.\n\nRedcaps sometimes work with evil fey such as grimstalkers (see their entry in this book), but such alliances are usually short-lived.\n\nHunting bands are not uncommon, especially at night, and during these hunts redcaps are most often encountered carrying their poisoned daggers (which they use to fell opponents larger or stronger than themselves).\n\nThey enjoy traveling into civilized areas and stealing livestock and pets, just to see the sorrowful looks on the faces of the farmers and children the next day when they find their animals missing or slaughtered.\n\nRedcaps prefer to dress in greens, browns, or other drab colors, except their hats. They gruesomely display, their long blood-soaked soaked caps, turned deep crimson to have slain. Generally, the avoid combat, preferring to harass those they encounter until such time as the redcap can strike with the advantage (such as against a sleeping or helpless opponent).\n\nThey normally throw stones or poisoned daggers at opponents before turning invisible and dashing away. Sleeping or helpless opponents are attacked with dagger or short sword, both of which are usually poisoned.\n\nIf cornered or threatened, a redcap turns invisible and attempts to slip away.\n\nAconite Poison: Type injury; save DC 15; frequency 1/ round for 6 rounds; effect 1d2 Constitution damage; cure 2 consecutive saves.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"4 doses of aconite poison; Other Gear dagger"},{"name":"Ronus","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"magical beast","init":4,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +3 natural","hp":22,"hd":"3d10+6","hdvalue":3,"fort":5,"ref":7,"will":3,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":60}],"melee":"bite +7 (1d6+3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":16,"dexterity":19,"constitution":15,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":14,"charisma":7,"baseatk":3,"cmb":6,"cmd":20,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Stealth","value":12}],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["pack (4-9)","lair (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A horrific creature steps forth, its body that of a great gray wolf and its head resembling a tremendous falcon covered with light brown feathers.  It sits poised to pounce, gnashing its great black, razor-sharp beak.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"When the sound of a falcon's shriek and a wolf's howl mixed together pierce the night air, chances are good that a solitary or pack of ronus are nearby and just about to strike. Turning and running only incites the pack for they know that no normal creature can outrun them (without the aid of magic).\n\nRonus always hunt in large packs. A solitary encounter with a ronus is almost unheard of. In such events, the creature encountered is most certainly injured, sick, or old. Such a creature will not attack, unless provoked and only fights to defend itself.\n\nRonus live by hunting and are carnivorous monsters. They will eat just about anything from mice to rabbits to goblins to ogres; anything they can catch and kill. Prey is attacked quickly and dispersed as quickly as possible before it has a chance to get its bearings and mount an attack against the ronus pack. Kills are eaten where they fall. The pack ravenously devours most of the meat, but don't pick the bones clean.\n\nArea scavengers appreciate this and some actually follow a ronus pack waiting for its chance to eat.\n\nRonus packs build their lairs in hard to find areas and often use leaves, fallen trees, and broken tree limbs to camouflage the lair. Ronus are social creatures and a lair typically contains 1-4 packs. If young are present, there will be 2d4 of them; they are noncombatants.\n\nRonus are fast and use their quickness to their advantage when hunting.\n\nThey often sight their prey from a great distance and rely on their closing speed to catch it before it notices the ronus and has a chance to flee. Prey that tries to escape is run down; very few creatures can outrun a hungry ronus.\n\nThese creatures also use \"flush\" tactics to force prey into a trap. For example, a pack may chase their prey for a short distance before most of the pack seems to break off and give up. One or two continue pursuit, forcing the prey to turn this way or that way; in essence, forcing the prey to go the way the ronus want it to go. Prey that fall for this tactic often wind up face-to-face with the rest of the ronus pack.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Ryven","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"monstrous humanoid","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 natural","hp":22,"hd":"3d8+9","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":6,"will":4,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"short sword +6 (1d6+2) or 2 claws +6 (1d4+2), bite +6 (1d6+2)","ranged":"light crossbow +6 (1d8, 19-20/x2, range 80 ft.)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rage"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":17,"constitution":17,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":12,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmd":18,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Survival","value":1,"special":"+5 scent tracking"}],"languages":["Common"],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","gang (2-5)","raiding party (4-7 plus 1 leader of 3rd-4th level)","tribe (10-20 plus 50% noncombatants plus two 3rd-level sergeants, one 5th-level lieutenant, one 4th-level shaman, 1 leader of 5th-8th level, and 3-4 dire badgers)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This humanoid resembles an anthropomorphic badger. Standing the size of man, his body is much broader and covered with grayish fur, colored with a white tribe running from his face and down his back to his bushy tail. His hands and ft. end in elongated, sharpened claws.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Rage","kind":"Ex","details":"A ryven that takes damage in combat flies into a berserk rage on its next turn, clawing and biting madly until it or its opponent is dead. It gains Strength +4, Constitution +4, and -2 AC. The ryven cannot end its rage voluntarily, but can attempt a DC 20 Will save each round to regain."}],"description":"Ryven are known as badger-folk by those of other races. In appearance, they resemble man-sized, bipedal badgers. They are a battlehardened race, warring mostly with elves or centaurs, and many ryvens carry the scars of such battles with pride. Ryven prefer to battle with their natural weapons, relying on their claws and fangs to down their foes. Some carry weapons, usually short swords, daggers, and light crossbows. More insidious ryven sometimes coat their weapons or crossbow bolts with death adder poison. Ryvens prefer to attack from surprise. Often, a ryven burrows underneath an unsuspecting foe and emerges from the ground in a blast of rock and dirt, catching the opponent by surprise. It then tears into its opponent with teeth and claws. Ryven have managed to gain some control over their animalistic behavior and when angered and raging, can attempt to end the rage. Ryven Society Ryven are tribal folk and build large burrows and tunnels that travel deep into the ground. Leaders are always the strongest and largest of a tribe. Tribes rarely number more than 20 creatures, so it isn't uncommon to find more than one tribe sharing the same burrow. Ryvens are carnivores and actively hunt sources of meat. Favorite meals include sheep, goat, deer, and reptiles such as snakes, especially vipers. Ryvens are not adverse to eating humanoid flesh and sometimes dine on captured enemies. They consume massive quantities of meat, much more than one would normally expect a creature of their size and stature to consume, so most tribes keep a ready supply of meat handy. They are nocturnal creatures so most encounters occur at night. Raiding parties also conduct their business at night, seeking out those who would camp or sleep too close to the ryven's lair. Such unfortunates are either robbed or killed, or both. The goods and gear of slain foes are carried back to the burrow and stored. If the raiding party is also on a hunting excursion, slain foes are likewise carried back to the lair and stored until they are eaten. Entrances and exits to a ryven burrow are well camouflaged so enemies and predators cannot locate easily them. Each burrow consists of many interlocking passageways (most large enough to allow the ryvens to walk upright) and chambers. Such chambers function as living quarters, storage, battle grounds, or temples for the tribe. Temples are considered highly sacred places to a ryven tribe and are always well-protected and guarded. Most ryven tribes keep company with badgers or dire badgers.","as_character":[{"description":"","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+4 Strength, +6 Dexterity, +6 Constitution, -4 Intelligence, +2 Wisdom. Strong and agile, ryven characters make excellent rogues or fighters. Each ryven tribe has at least one shaman, usually a druid. Ryven clerics worship Domga, \"the Badger God\" and can choose any two of the following domains: Animal, Evil (if evil), Plant, Trickery, or Strength."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Ryvens can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Low-Light Vision","value":"Ryvens see twice as far as humans in conditions of dim light."},{"type":"Scent","value":"Rivens gain the scent special ability"},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"A ryven begins with three levels of monstrous humanoid, which provides 3d8 HD, a base attack bonus of +3, and base save bonuses of Fort +1, Ref +3, and Will +3."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A ryven's monstrous humanoid levels give it skill points equal to 6 x (2 + Int modifier, minimum 1). Its class skills are Escape Artist and Perception."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A ryven's monstrous humanoid class levels give it two feats."},{"type":"Born Tracker","value":"+4 racial bonus to tracking by scent."},{"type":"Slippery","value":"+4 racial bonus to Escape Artist checks."},{"type":"Natural AC","value":"a rivens tough hide provides it with a +1 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Common. A riven with high Intelligence scores can choose from the following bonus languages: Elven, Goblin, Sylvan, Orc."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Sandling","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"outsider","subtypes":["elemental","extraplanar","earth"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"tremorsense"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +7 natural, -1 size","hp":26,"hd":"4d10+4","hdvalue":4,"fort":5,"ref":2,"will":4,"dr":"5/bludgeoning","immunities":["elemental traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to water"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"burrow","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +6 (1d8+4)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":17,"dexterity":13,"constitution":13,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":11,"charisma":11,"baseatk":4,"cmb":8,"cmd":19,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":10}],"environments":["any (Elemental Plane of Earth)"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature appears to be a large snake formed of earth and sand. A slit seems to function as the creature's mouth.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Vulnerability to Water","kind":"Ex","details":"For every 2 gallons of water that hit a sandling, it is slowed (as the slow spell) for 1 round (no save)."}],"description":"Sandlings are creatures from the Elemental Plane of Earth. A sandling in its natural form resembles a mound of sand that covers a 10-foot area.\n\nThey are most often summoned to the Material Plane by clerics and wizards, though on occasion a sandling slips through a vortex connecting the Elemental Plane of Earth to the Material Plane. Sandlings live on a diet of minerals only and cannot digest plants, herbs, meat, or other substances. Opponents killed by a sandling are left for scavengers.\n\nSandlings have no real society and are highly solitary in nature. Though they harbor no ill-will towards others of their kind, it is very rare to find more than one sandling operating near another. Reproduction methods among sandlings is unknown to sages but it is believed they create others of their kind by division (that is, an adult sandling splits into two or more smaller creatures).\n\nA sandling lies in wait in its natural form until it detects an interloper in its territory. It then assumes its serpentine form and attacks with an abrasive bite.\n\nA typical sandling is 10 ft. long but can grow to a length of 20 to 25 ft.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Sandling originally appeared in the First Edition module A4 In the Dungeons of the Slave Lords (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and later in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Scarlet Spider Swarm","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Fine","type":"vermin","subtypes":["swarm"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":20,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":18,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +8 size, +0 natural","hp":13,"hd":"3d8","hdvalue":3,"fort":3,"ref":3,"will":1,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects","weapon damage","swarm traits","vermin traits"],"weaknesses":["swarm traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"climb","speed":10}],"melee":"swarm (1d6 plus disease)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"disease"},{"type":"distraction","details":"DC 11"},{"type":"painful bite"}],"strength":1,"dexterity":15,"constitution":10,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":2,"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":10},{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Stealth","value":22}],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-4)","colony (5-10)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A whirling storm of scarlet-banded spiders scuttles toward you.","group":"Scarlet Spider","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Disease","kind":"Ex","details":"Red Ache: Swarm-injury; save Fort DC 13; onset 1d3 days; frequency 1/day; effect 1d6 Str damage; cure 2 consecutive saves. The save DC is Constitutionbased and includes a +2 racial bonus."},{"type":"Painful Bite","kind":"Ex","details":"The bite of a scarlet spider is extremely painful. A creature bitten takes a -1 circumstance penalty on attack rolls, weapon damage rolls, and ability and skill checks until cured. A neutralize poison or remove disease spell, or a DC 13 Heal check removes the penalty. The check DC is Constitution-based and includes a +2 racial bonus."}],"description":"Scarlet spiders dwell in temperate forests and make their homes in the hollows of trees, under fall trees, and in dense foliage.\n\nScarlet spiders are deadly, aggressive black spiders about 3 inches long. Their legs are long, thin, and covered in a thin layer of dark hair.\n\nTheir bodies are solid black with horizontal bands of scarlet ringing them. Scarlet spiders are not web-spinning spiders; therefore, they don't possess the standard web ability of other spiders.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Screaming Devilkin","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"LE","size":"Small","type":"magical beast","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":15,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 dodge, +4 natural, +1 size","hp":16,"hd":"3d10","hdvalue":3,"fort":3,"ref":6,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":5},{"type":"fly","speed":30,"maneuverability":"poor"}],"melee":"tail-barb +7 (1d4)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"scream"}],"strength":10,"dexterity":17,"constitution":10,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":11,"charisma":12,"baseatk":3,"cmb":2,"cmd":16,"feats":[{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":5},{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11}],"languages":["Infernal"],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-5)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This winged creature is humanoid in appearance with frail and spindly arms and legs. It has a long, thick tail that ends in a wicked barb.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Scream","kind":"Su","details":"A screaming devilkin can howl continuously as a free action. This painful howling affects all creatures within 60 ft. that hear it. Affected creatures must succeed on a DC 12 Fortitude save or be dazed for one round. The save DC is Charisma-based. The effects of multiple screaming devilkin using their scream ability in an overlapping area isn't cumulative; only one save need be made each round. Conversation, even shouting, is inaudible within 60 ft. of a screaming devilkin using this ability. Spellcasters in the area must succeed on a Concentration check (DC 15 + spell level) each time they try to cast a spell. Silence negates the devilkin's scream for the duration of the spell."}],"description":"Shrill wails echoing through the night signal the arrival of a screaming devilkin. Screaming devilkins are smallish beasts, humanoid in appearance but with frail and spindly arms and legs. These weak limbs are nearly useless for combat and locomotion, but the screaming devilkin makes up for this disability with its bat-like wings. Although screaming devilkins are fast fliers, they are not particularly agile on the wing. Screaming devilkins also have a long, muscular, barbed tail that is their primary means of physical attack. It should be noted that despite its appearance and name, the screaming devilkin has no connection with outsiders of any type.\n\nA screaming devilkin is a tireless combatant, refusing to back down regardless of how the combat is progressing; it always attacks on sight.\n\nIt attacks in melee by stabbing with its barbed tail (inflicting piercing damage) and screams continuously until it or its opponent is dead.\n\nA typical screaming devilkin is 3 ft. tall and has a wingspan of about 5 ft. Its skin is reddish-brown in color and its eyes are black. Its tail is about 2-1/2 ft. long.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Screaming Devilkin originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Shadow Hunter Hatchling","cr":2,"xp":600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"magical beast","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"30 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"30 ft."}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +2 natural","hp":22,"hd":"3d10+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":4,"will":1,"defensive_abilities":["shadowblend"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"climb","speed":10},{"type":"swim","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +6 (1d4+4 plus poison plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":16,"dexterity":13,"constitution":12,"intelligence":5,"wisdom":10,"charisma":3,"baseatk":3,"cmb":6,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":17,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Ability Focus (poison)"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6,"special":"+16 in dimly lit & unlit areas"},{"skill":"Swim","value":11}],"sq":[{"type":"hunt by scent"}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","nest (5-8 hatchlings)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"The shadow hunter is a great, dark serpent that dwells in deep caverns beneath the earth, where it hunts dark elves and other Medium to Large sized creatures. An adult specimen is over 40 ft. long and nearly 5 ft. thick in its midsection. In bright light it can be seen to be covered with non-reflective black scales, and its underbelly is the dark red of clotted blood. Shadow hunters have the supernatural ability to blend in with shadows, both to protect themselves and to stalk and ambush prey. Unlike normal snakes, shadow hunters often work in groups of two or three to corner prey in passages.","group":"Shadow Hunter","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Bite-injury; save DC 14 Fort; frequency 1/ round for 6 rounds; effect 1d4 Constitution damage; cure 2 consecutive saves. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Hunt by Scent","kind":"Ex","details":"Shadow hunters are expert at tracking prey through the dim warrens where they dwell. They can track using their Perception skill in place of Survival."},{"type":"Shadowblend","kind":"Su","details":"In areas of dim and no light, shadow hunters gain improved concealment; there is a 40% miss chance when attacking one in such conditions."}],"description":"Shadow hunters generally prefer to hunt in networks of twisting passages that allow them to move around their intended prey, or even approach it from multiple directions. They are particularly fond of elf flesh, but eats any Small to Large creature as long as it is living, organic, and animal-based (i.e., not a plant or fungus). When they attack, they prefer to strike and envenom their prey, holding on and chewing the poison into their opponent until it stops struggling. If there is more than one foe present, they don't try to grab their prey, preferring to strike at those that threaten it, retreating if need be to return later to consume their hopefully dead prey.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Originally appearing in Rappan Athuk Reloaded (© Necromancer Games, 2006)"}];
